==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|abaisshment}}, from {{der|en|frm|abaissement|t=astonishment}} alteration of {{m|frm|esbaissement}}, from {{m|frm|esbaiss}} + {{m|frm|-ment}}.<ref>{{R:MW3 1976|page=2}}</ref> Compare {{cog|fr|ébahissement}}. Equivalent to {{af|en|abash|-ment}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əˈbæʃ.mənt/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/əˈbæʃ.mənt/|a=GenAm}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vininn126-abashment.wav|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The state of being [[abashed]]; embarrassment from [[shame]]. {{defdate|First attested from 1350 to 1470.}}<ref>{{R:SOED5|page=2}}</ref>
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1540|translator=w:Myles Coverdale|title={{w|Great Bible|The Byble in Englyshe}}|location=London|publisher=Thomas Berthelet|section={{w|Deuteronomy}} 28[.28]|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A10405.0001.001
|passage=And the lorde shall smyte the with madnesse, and blyndnesse & '''abashment''' of herte.}}
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|3|8|page=521|text=For her faint hart was with the frosen cold<br>Benumbd so inly, that her wits nigh fayld,<br>And all her sences with '''abashment''' quite were quayld.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1768|author={{w|Henry Brooke (writer)|Henry Brooke}}|title=w:The Fool of Quality|location=Dublin|volume=3|chapter=13|pages=35–36|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004836069.0001.003
|passage=On my appearing her Spirits again took the Alarm. She scarce ventured a Glance toward me. I was greatly pained by the '''Abashment''' under which I saw she laboured, and I hastened to relieve myself as well as her from the Distress.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1940|author={{w|Richard Wright (author)|Richard Wright}}|title=w:Native Son|location=London|publisher=Jonathan Cape|year_published=1970|section=Book 2, p. 185|url=https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.499539/page/n221
|passage=“Did he say he would let you meet some white women if you joined the reds?”<br>He knew that sex relations between blacks and whites were repulsive to most white men.<br>“Nawsuh,” he said, simulating '''abashment'''.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2014|author={{w|Don Gutteridge (writer)|Don Gutteridge}}|title=Death of a Patriot|url=https://archive.org/details/deathofpatriotma0000gutt|chapter=8|page=104|publisher=Simon & Schuster|location=New York
|passage={{...}} Marc, who well knew the pangs and '''abashments''' of romantic love, recognized the emotions here as genuine and heartfelt and was encouraged.}}

